=== KNOWLEDGE BASE (full content of the site) ===

[SECTION: Overview]
LlmAvatarAssistant is a drop-in React component that floats a 3D avatar on top
of any single-page app. It asks a llama.cpp (or any OpenAI-compatible) model a
question, shows the answer in a comic-style speech bubble, spins the avatar
while "thinking", and — when the answer mentions a section of the page — scrolls
the page to that section automatically.

[SECTION: Features]
- Transparent background — only the model, input and bubble show.
- Configurable endpoint: URL, API key, model, temperature.
- Swappable 3D model loaded from a file (GLB), auto-fit to size.
- Idle + "thinking" (circular spin) animations.
- Streaming responses with a scrollable speech bubble.
- Automatic section detection & smooth scroll in the host SPA.

[SECTION: Quick Start]
Drop it into any React app. Usage:
    import LlmAvatarAssistant from 'LlmAvatarAssistant';
    <LlmAvatarAssistant
      config={{
        baseUrl: 'http://192.168.1.2:8080/v1',
        apiKey: 'your-key',
        model: 'your-model-id',
        defaultText: 'Hi! Ask me about this page.',
        modelUrl: '/models/robot.glb',
        side: 'right',
      }}
    />

[SECTION: Configuration]
Every prop can be overridden via `config`:
- baseUrl — OpenAI-compatible endpoint (llama.cpp `/v1`).
- apiKey — Bearer token; empty when the server is unsecured.
- model — Model id sent in the payload.
- defaultText — Text shown before the first question.
- modelUrl — Path to the GLB avatar file.
- side — `left` or `right`.
- sectionDiscovery — `auto` or an explicit section list.
- systemPrompt — Persona + instructions to the model.

[SECTION: The 3D Avatar]
The avatar is rendered with three.js. On load the model is measured (bounding
box) and scaled to fit the fixed viewport, so any GLB — big, small, or offset —
looks proportionate. While a query is running the whole model orbits a circle;
at rest it idles. If the file fails to load, a built-in placeholder robot keeps
the component functional.

[SECTION: Auto-Scroll Behavior]
After each answer the component scans it for the title (or alias) of an on-page
section. On a match it smoothly scrolls the host page to that section and
highlights the matched text in the bubble. Sections are discovered
automatically from `<section id>` / `<h2 id>`, or supplied explicitly.

[SECTION: Testing]
The component ships with unit tests (Vitest) covering the LLM client, the
section scanner and the auto-fit math, plus end-to-end tests (Playwright) that
run the real component against a live llama.cpp router and assert that an answer
referencing a section scrolls the page.

[SECTION: License]
Released under the MIT License. The bundled 3D model is three.js
"RobotExpressive" (public domain / CC0), so the project is fully free to use and
redistribute.

=== END OF KNOWLEDGE BASE ===
